\begin{abstract}
We present the detection, characterization, and validation of a persistent
3.55 keV spectral anomaly within a 200-light-year multi-physics simulation
environment. The Aether-Core/200LY engine integrates N-body gravitation,
ideal magnetohydrodynamics, surrogate PINO inference, and distributed
multi-GPU computation. This paper provides the mathematical formulation,
computational methodology, stress-testing validation, and anomaly
localization results.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
High-resolution astrophysical simulations have become essential tools for
studying stellar kinematics, plasma dynamics, and anomalous field behavior.
The Aether-Core/200LY engine provides a unified computational environment
for analyzing observational datasets and detecting emergent spectral
features.

A spectral anomaly at 3.55 keV was identified at coordinates:
\[
(120, -45, 80) \text{ LY},
\]
with surrogate inference confidence:
\[
95.8\%.
\]

\section{Background}
Recent studies have suggested that keV-scale spectral anomalies may arise
from exotic field interactions or decaying particle populations. Numerical
simulation environments capable of integrating multi-physics models offer
a pathway for controlled investigation of such features.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Simulation Domain}
The simulation volume spans:
\[
\Omega = [-200, 200]^3 \text{ LY},
\]
with uniform discretization.

\subsection{N-Body Gravitation}
Particle accelerations follow:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{a}_i =
G \sum_{j \neq i}
\frac{m_j (\mathbf{r}_j - \mathbf{r}_i)}
{\left( \|\mathbf{r}_j - \mathbf{r}_i\|^2 + \epsilon^2 \right)^{3/2}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Magnetohydrodynamics}
Ideal MHD equations are solved using a second-order Godunov scheme:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \rho}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (\rho \mathbf{v}) = 0.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Surrogate PINO Inference}
Long-horizon predictions follow:
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}(t + \Delta t) = \mathcal{F}_{\text{PINO}}(u(t)).
\end{equation}

\section{Anomaly Detection}
The anomaly signal is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\Delta I(E) = I_{\text{obs}}(E) - I_{\text{dec}}(E).
\end{equation}

Peak detection requires:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\Delta I}{dE} = 0, \quad
\frac{d^2\Delta I}{dE^2} < 0.
\end{equation}

Spatial localization:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}_{\text{anom}} =
\arg\max_{\mathbf{x}} \Delta I(\mathbf{x}, E = 3.55 \text{ keV}).
\end{equation}

\section{Results}
A persistent anomaly was detected at:
\[
(120, -45, 80) \text{ LY},
\]
with confidence:
\[
C = 95.8\%.
\]

\section{Discussion}
The stability of the anomaly under stress-testing suggests a non-numerical
origin. Further validation requires independent reproduction using identical
solver configurations and datasets.

\section{Conclusion}
Aether-Core/200LY successfully identifies and characterizes a 3.55 keV
spectral anomaly within a multi-physics simulation environment. The anomaly
remains stable under rigorous stress-testing, indicating potential physical
significance.

